\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Alice prepares $\rho _i \in \{ \rho _1, \ldots , \rho _N \}$ and sends it to Bob. Bob wins the game if he correctly identifies the index $i$. }}{5}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Alice prepares $\rho _i \in \{ \rho _1, \ldots , \rho _N \}$ and sends it to Bob. Bob wins the game if he correctly \emph {excludes} an index $j \neq i$. }}{8}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Alice prepares the state $\ket {\psi _x} = \ket {\psi _{x_1}} \otimes \cdots \otimes \ket {\psi _{x_n}}$ and sends it to Bob. Bob's goal is to compute a Boolean function $f(x)$. Bob can either store all of his received states and then perform a joint measurement (left panel (a)) to obtain a guess $b$ for $f(x)$, or he can measure each of his received states as soon as it is received using predetermined local measurements to obtain a guess $y_i$ for $x_i$ which can be collectively used to guess $f(x)$ (right panel (b)). }}{11}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces Circuit for the swap test which can learn the inner product between two (perhaps unknown) states $\ket {\psi }$ and $\ket {\phi }$, by approximating $|\braket {\psi }{\phi }|$ from many measured samples. }}{19}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Circuit for the Hadamard test to learn the inner product between two (perhaps unknown) states $\ket {\psi }$ and $\ket {\phi }$. When $b = 0$, the Hadamard test approximates from many samples $\Re (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$, and when $b = 1$, $\Im (\braket {\psi }{\phi })$ can be approximated instead. The unitary $U$ maps the state $\ket {\psi }$ to the state $\ket {\phi }$. }}{20}{figure.caption.24}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Approximate ground-space entanglement measure as a function of the transverse field $h$ for $H_{\mathrm {Ising}}$ with 12 qubits in panel~(a) and 14 qubits in panel~(b), with $J=1$ and $g=0$. In each case, $\mathcal {A}$ consists of the first half of the qubits and $\mathcal {B}$ consists of the second half. Both $\lambda _{\max }$ and $\lambda _{\min }$ can be computed for these graphs. }}{38}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces The error generated by our heuristic as the number of ansatz states is varied for the Ising Hamiltonian on 12 qubits (with $J=1$, $g=0$, and $h=1.3$). Here, $\hat {\alpha }_L$ and $\tilde {\alpha }_L$ denote, respectively, the maximum and mean of the $\hat {\alpha }_{L,i}$ values computed using 10 randomly selected reference states. }}{42}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Comparison of calculated values (where available) and approximations of the ground energy and separable ground energy for the Ising Hamiltonian ($J=1$, $g=0$, and $h=1.4$) for systems of up to 28 qubits. }}{44}{figure.caption.54}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Flowchart of the hybrid quantum-classical algorithm. The quantum stage computes the Gram matrix from state-preparation unitaries. The classical stage supplies the Gram matrix, reward matrix, and prior-probability vector to a reduced semidefinite program, whose solution gives the optimal reward. }}{68}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces A quantum source sends a stream of states $\ket {\psi }$, with two mutations occurring in this example. The task is to design an optimal detector that determines when the mutations occurred. }}{69}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Here we consider sequences in which the state $\ket {0}$ mutates to the state $\ket {+}$. Using the closer-the-better reward scheme with $\gamma = 1/\sqrt {2}$, panel~(a) shows the error between the heuristic \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} and the reduced SDP \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP}, while panel~(b) shows the runtimes of both methods. The difference between \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} and \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} decreases rapidly, approaching $10^{-3}$ at $N=220$. Although there is little timing advantage for sequences up to length $80$, computing \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} is roughly \emph {seven} times faster than computing \eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D'_1CP} for larger values of $N$. }}{74}{figure.caption.79}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces We consider a sequence of $N$ states that begins in $\ket {0}$ and may switch to $\ket {+}$ at an unknown position. The optimal reward, corresponding to the optimal probability of identifying the location of the change point, is plotted as a function of $N$ for different values of the closeness parameter $\mu $. The reward stabilizes for large sequence lengths, and larger values of $\mu $ substantially improve the reward, as expected. }}{75}{figure.caption.81}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces We consider the closer-the-better reward scheme with $\gamma = |\braket {\psi }{\phi }| = \cos (\theta )$ for $\theta \in \{\pi /8, \pi /4, 3\pi /8\}$, where $\ket {\psi } = \ket {0}$ and $\ket {\phi } = \cos (\theta )\ket {0} + \sin (\theta )\ket {1}$. Panel~(a) shows the reward Bob receives for the sequence $\ket {\psi }^{\otimes 50} \otimes \ket {\phi }^{\otimes 50}$ under this reward scheme. Panel~(b) shows the optimal reward function value as a function of $N$, the number of states sent. As the states become closer to one another, locating the change point becomes more difficult. However, the rewards are scaled so that more difficult settings correspond to larger rewards. }}{76}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces For a sequence of length $N=50$, we compare the difference between the heuristic~\eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} and the optimal SDP with the difference between Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch3_QSD-sentis2016bound} and the optimal SDP, as the value of $\gamma ^2 = |\braket {\psi }{\phi _1}|^2$ is varied. Although our heuristic provides a tighter bound than Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch3_QSD-sentis2016bound}, it requires solving an SDP, whereas Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch3_QSD-sentis2016bound} is a closed-form expression. }}{77}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces For a sequence of length $N=15$, we compare the difference between the heuristic~\eqref {prog:ch3_QSD-D''_1CP} and the optimal SDP with the difference between Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch3_QSD-sentis2017approx} and the optimal SDP for varying values of $\gamma $. For $\gamma \leq \gamma ^*=(\sqrt {5}-1)/2$, the error between the heuristic and the optimal SDP is of order $10^{-5}$. When $\gamma >\gamma ^*$, Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch3_QSD-sentis2016bound} provides a substantially tighter bound than the heuristic. }}{79}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces A quantum source sends an $n$-qubit state $\ket {\phi }$ through a single-qubit Pauli error channel. The task is to classify the type of error, $I$, $X$, $Z$, or $Y$, that affected the state without determining \emph {which qubit} the error acted on. The input state $\ket {\phi }$ need not be a separable state. }}{80}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces The optimal error-type classification probability for $\ket {\psi _n(\theta )} = \cos (\theta )\ket {\mathrm {GHZ}_n} + \sin (\theta )\ket {\mathrm {W}_n}$ as a function of $\theta \in [0,\pi /2]$. For $n=2$, the success probability is $\max \{\cos ^2(\theta ),\sin ^2(\theta )\}$. For $n\geq 3$, the curves appear to have an inflection point near $\theta =49.8^\circ $, corresponding to a success probability of approximately $0.907$. }}{85}{figure.caption.87}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces If given a trine state, each with equal probability, we have $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {Best}}=\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PGM}}=2/3$, with $\mathcal {P}_{\text {Random}}=1/3$ being the blind-guessing probability, and $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PBM}}=1/6$ approximating $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {Worst}}=0$. The pretty good measurement is given by the POVM $G_i=\frac {2}{3}| \psi _i \rangle \langle \psi _i |$; the pretty bad measurement is given by the POVM $B_i=\frac {1}{3}\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _{j\neq i}| \psi _j \rangle \langle \psi _j |$; and the worst measurement is given by $W_i=\frac {2}{3}| \psi _i^{\perp } \rangle \langle \psi _i^{\perp } |$, where $| \psi _i^{\perp } \rangle \langle \psi _i^{\perp } |$ is a $\pi /2$ rotation of $\ket {\psi _i}$. }}{95}{figure.caption.97}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Success probabilities of the pretty good measurement and pretty bad measurement for 1000 randomly generated pairs of qubit states with randomly chosen prior probabilities. The pretty good measurement always achieves a success probability greater than $1/2$, whereas the pretty bad measurement always achieves a success probability less than $1/2$. }}{97}{figure.caption.98}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Success probabilities of the pretty good measurement and pretty bad measurement for 1000 randomly generated ensembles of six qubit states with randomly selected prior probabilities. For small values of $n$, $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PGM}}$ appears to cluster around $\frac {2}{n}$, while $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PBM}}$ appears to cluster around $\frac {n-2}{n(n-1)}$. The case $n=6$ is shown here. }}{98}{figure.caption.99}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Probabilities for the quantum state discrimination task defined by $\ket {\psi _i} = \ket {0}^{\otimes (i-1)} \otimes \ket {\psi } \otimes \ket {0}^{\otimes (7-i)}$, where $\ket {\psi } = \gamma \ket {0} + \sqrt {1-\gamma ^2}\ket {1}$ and $i\in \{1,\ldots ,7\}$. While $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PGM}}=\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {Best}}$ for all values of $\gamma $, $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PBM}}$ begins to depart from the worst success probability near $\gamma =0.4$. All four probabilities coincide at $\gamma =1$, since all the states are identical in this case; see Eq.~\eqref {eq:ch4_PBM-allthesame}. }}{99}{figure.caption.100}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Given one of the equally likely states $\ket {\psi _i} = \ket {0}^{\otimes (i-1)} \otimes \ket {\psi } \otimes \ket {0}^{\otimes (7-i)}$, where $\ket {\psi } = \gamma \ket {0} + \sqrt {1-\gamma ^2}\ket {1}$ and $i\in \{1,\ldots ,7\}$, we plot the probability of successfully excluding the anomaly $\ket {\psi }$ using the PBM and the probability of successfully identifying $\ket {\psi }$ using the PGM. The probability $\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PGM}}$ decreases rapidly beginning near $\gamma =0.2$ and approaches the blind identification probability of $1/7$ at $\gamma =1$. In contrast, the exclusion success probability $1-\mathcal {P}_{\mathrm {PBM}}$ decreases more slowly and approaches the blind exclusion probability of $6/7$ at $\gamma =1$. As expected, the exclusion success probability is substantially greater than the identification success probability. }}{100}{figure.caption.101}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The three-dimensional hypercube $Q_3 \cong Q_2 \mathbin {\square }K_2$, visualized as two copies of $Q_2$ with a perfect matching, shown by dashed edges, connecting corresponding vertices that differ in the last-bit coordinate. }}{129}{figure.caption.115}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Greedy and global success probabilities for the $\mathrm {AND}$ function. As $n$ increases, both strategies approach perfect success exponentially quickly, and the gap between them decreases rapidly. }}{134}{figure.caption.126}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Greedy and global success probabilities for the majority function as functions of the overlap between the two single-qubit states. }}{138}{figure.caption.133}%
